  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers with a few embedded thunderstorms overspread \nthe region this morning. Pockets of torrential rainfall bring the \npotential for localized flash flooding with the greatest risk near \nthe south coast. \n\nA fairly strong shortwave and associated mid-level warm front will \napproach the region this morning. In response...the atmosphere will \nundergo quite an instability burst with the Showalter indices \ndropping nearly 20 units inside 12 hours. This is often a very \nstrong signal for a cluster of showers and a few embedded \nthunderstorms along with the potential for localized torrential \nrainfall. Pwats on the NAM/GFS climb to near 2.25 inches across \nRI/SE MA today...which is extremely high for this part of the \ncountry. In fact...the EPS situational awareness table has those \nPwat values outside the model/s climatology for this time of year. \nIn addition...the HREF/s worst case scenario indicates modest \nprobabilities for 6 HR rainfall to exceed the 10 year ARI/s for \nRI/SE MA. Lastly...the HREF continues to indicate a 30 percent \nprobability for 3\"+ of rain inside 3 hours across RI/SE MA. That is \noften another signal to look for in the potential for flash \nflooding. Keep in mind though...these are just favorable ingredients \nand mesoscale factors will determine the outcome.  \n\nThe above ingredients are favorable to produce a narrow swath of \ntorrential rainfall and potentially localized flash flooding...But \nonly if the mesoscale environment cooperates. That is often very \nhard to forecast even in the near term. In this case...the track of \na meso-low and its surface boundary will likely be the main player. \nIf it tracks near the south coast and a surface boundary enhances \nconvergence with the extreme Pwat plume in place...the risk for \ntorrential rainfall and localized flash flooding will increase. \nCurrent guidance is still favoring RI/SE MA and especially areas \nnear the south coast at the greatest risk for this occurrence. \nHowever...the current state of the science still prevents us to pin \nan exact area so we can not rule out this risk further north even \ninto the Boston and Worcester metro areas. And this is still only a \nwatch...so if the mesoscale environment does not setup the band of \ntorrential rainfall and localized flash flooding will not occur. \n\nLastly the overall severe weather threat is quite low today given \nmost of the instability will be elevated. However...there is a very \nlow risk for a brief spin-up/waterspout in the vicinity of the \nmesoscale low and its associated triple point. Again...it is a \nrather low probability but something we need to watch in these \nsetups. \n\nSo to sum up...The Flood Watch continues for the BOS-ORH corridor \npoints south and east with the highest risk near the south coast. \nThe potential exists for 2-4\" of rain inside 3 hours within the \nFlood Watch. Outside that region...rainfall amounts of 0.50\" to \n1.50\" seem reasonable. \n\nThe system itself is fairly progressive...so expect the bulk of the \nsteady showers to come to an end from northwest to southeast roughly \nbetween 2 and 4 pm. However...some of the steady activity may linger \nuntil 5-6 pm towards the Cape and Islands. A cold front will likely \nbring a round of brief scattered showers very late tonight into \nearly Mon morning...but not the widespread heavy rainfall we will be \ndealing with today. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Overall the weather looks rather quiet and mainly \ndry for the upcoming work week with just the risk for a few showers \nsometime Thu and/or Fri. Seasonable temps Mon and Tue with a brief \nbout of summer warmth possible Wed and/or Thu ahead of a cold front. \n\nAny lingering scattered showers depart the southeast New England \ncoast by mid-morning Monday behind the cold front. Otherwise...high \npressure builds back into the region Mon and Tue bringing dry and \nseasonable weather. High temps will mainly be in the upper 60s to \nthe middle 70s with lows in the 40s and 50s both days. This high \npressure system moves east of the region by Wed. This may allow for \na brief return of summerlike warmth Wed and/or Thu. Much of the \nupcoming work week looks dry...but the next cold front may bring a \nfew showers to the region sometime Thu and/or Fri but it does not \nlook significant at this time.",
